# 1765. Map of Highest Peak

- **LeetCode:** https://leetcode.com/problems/map-of-highest-peak/
- **Topic:** Graph, Matrix, Multi-Source BFS
- **Language:** Java
- **Status:** Solved

## 1. My Intuition

We need to assign heights to every cell such that:
- Water cells have height 0.
- Adjacent cells have a height difference of at most 1.
- The maximum possible height is achieved.

I recognized that this problem could be solved using Multi-Source BFS.

## 2. My Approach

1. Add all water cells to the queue and assign height 0.
2. Initialize all land cells with -1 to indicate unvisited cells.
3. Start BFS from all water cells simultaneously.
4. Explore all four directions.
5. If a neighboring cell is unvisited, assign its height as the current cell's height + 1.
6. Add the newly visited cell to the queue.
7. Return the height matrix.

## 3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

No major difficulty with identifying the algorithm.

The important part was understanding why assigning the shortest distance from water to each cell produces the maximum possible peak height.

## 4. What Clicked

The height of each cell is equal to its shortest distance from the nearest water cell.

Multi-Source BFS guarantees these shortest distances in an unweighted grid.

## 5. Final Submitted Code

class Solution {
    public int[][] highestPeak(int[][] isWater) {
        int m = isWater.length;
        int n = isWater[0].length;

        int[][] height = new int[m][n];
        Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (isWater[i][j] == 1) {
                    height[i][j] = 0;
                    q.offer(new int[]{i, j});
                } else {
                    height[i][j] = -1;
                }
            }
        }

        int[] dr = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int[] dc = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int[] curr = q.poll();
            int r = curr[0];
            int c = curr[1];

            for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
                int nr = r + dr[k];
                int nc = c + dc[k];

                if (nr >= 0 && nr < m &&
                    nc >= 0 && nc < n &&
                    height[nr][nc] == -1) {

                    height[nr][nc] = height[r][c] + 1;
                    q.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }

        return height;
    }
}

## 6. Complexity Analysis

- Time Complexity: O(m * n)
- Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(m * n)

## 7. Revision Notes

- Identify all water cells as sources.
- Initialize all sources before starting BFS.
- Use -1 to identify unvisited cells.
- Height = shortest distance from the nearest water cell.
- Every cell is visited at most once.
